! C22-6578 PROBLEM 4.4, "COMPRESSION OF SPARSE VECTOR", printed pp.37-38
! (PDF 38-39), Method 1, read in the ink at 300 dpi.
!
!   "Given a sparse vector of N components stored in consecutive
!   floating-point words beginning at SVEC.  It has a bit image stored in
!   consecutive bits beginning at the full word beginning at BIMAGE.
!   Compress the vector into the smallest possible storage space on the
!   basis of this bit image, and put the result in consecutive words
!   beginning at SVEC also."
!
! THE SEVENTEEN PRINTED STATEMENTS FROM "LX, $1, XW1; LX, $3, XW3" TO "XW3
! XW, 0.0, N, $" ARE AS PRINTED, assembled by the host STRAP II
! (ASM=strap2), the label END on "Z, SVEC($3)" included (strap2 takes a
! location-field END as a label; the deck's own "END, START" is the
! pseudo-op).  The deck adds: START on the first line, for END; N, which
! the page never defines (N SYN,24); the data, between two guards.
! Readings: "SVEC ($2)" is printed with a blank, squeezed out; "0.01($1)"
! is bit 1, the one-bit step, as strap2 assembles it (the CONN word,
! at 35.32, holds address 000001).
!
! WHAT THE PROGRAM DOES, by the Reference Manual (22-6530-2, printed
! page = PDF - 3):
!   $1 = BIMAGE, count N+1; $3 = 0.0, count N, refill itself; LVNI,$2,1.0
!   gives $2 the value -1.0, count and refill untouched (p.25).
!   CONN  C0011(BU,1)(V+IC),0.01($1)   image bit i into accumulator bit
!         127 (connective 0011 is m, Figure 20, p.76; offset 0); RZ is
!         on if the result is zero (p.76); progressive indexing adds 0.01
!         to $1 and counts it down (p.21), setting XCZ (p.23).
!   BXCZ, END   after the (N+1)th test: N bits are tested, then bit N,
!         past the image, is read once more and END is taken.
!   V+I,$2,1.0  $2 = i words (V+I sets index indicators, not RZ).
!   BZRZ, LOWF  bit one: LWF(U), SVEC($2) -- "the same as LOAD except
!         that the data flag bits T, U, and V of the addressed operand
!         sign byte set the flag bits in positions 5-7 of the accumulator
!         sign byte register" (p.89) -- and ST(U), SVEC($3), which stores
!         bits 0-59, the sign and the flag bits 5-7 into 60-63 (p.89, Store),
!         so a word moves whole, flags and an unnormalized fraction
!         included; V+IC,$3,1.0 steps the output and counts it.
!   END   Z, SVEC($3); CB+,$3,END   STORE ZERO (p.31) over the N - m words
!         left, $3 counting from N - m to 0 (p.43).
!
! >>> A DENSE VECTOR (ALL N BITS ONE) IS NOT HANDLED: NOT AN ERRATUM IN
! THE INK, A CASE THE PAGE DOES NOT EXCLUDE.  $3's count is then 0 at
! END: Z stores into SVEC+N, past the vector, and CB+ counts 0 down to
! 2**18 - 1 (an 18-bit field, p.43) and branches, zeroing storage upward
! until it reaches something it cannot store.  NOT RUN; the verifier
! asserts the data is not dense.
!
! THE DATA AND THE EXPECTED VALUES, worked by
! tests/verify_problem_4_matrix.py from the words as strap2 assembles them
! and the statement, not by the model:
!   BIMAGE (16)9871498200400001: bits 0, 3, 4, 9, 10, 11, 15, 17, 20, 23
!   one among bits 0-23 (m = 10), and bits 24, 30, 41, 63 past N.
!   SVEC, 24 words: ten non-zero words where the image is one, written
!   in hex: 1.5; -5 with flag T; .5 with V; an unnormalized fraction;
!   -.140625 with U; all three flags; 2.75; a negative unnormalized
!   fraction; -1 with U and V; and, last, a negative word with T and U.
!   Where the image is zero: zero words, and two order-of-magnitude
!   zeros with flags (words 5 and 13), which the image calls zero.
!   Expected:
!     SVEC+0..9      the ten words, in order, flags and all
!     SVEC+10..23    0, by Z
!     $1             BIMAGE + 25 bits, count 0, refill XW1:
!                    (16)0032190000000028
!     $2             value 23.0, count 0, refill 0: (16)0005C00000000000
!     $3             value 24.0, count 0, refill XW3: (16)0006000000000029
!     location 7     the last connect, image bit 24 = 1: ones 1, left
!                    zeros 0: (16)0000000000002000
!     word 8         SVEC[23]'s bits 0-59, bits 60-63 still 0 (p.88):
!                    (16)00C9C00000000000
!     word 9         1: image bit 24 at accumulator bit 127
!     word 10, $SB   sign 1, flags T U from SVEC[23]: (16)0E00000000000000
!     the guards and the halt at the printed BEW.
!
! PASSED AGAINST THE MODEL AS IT STOOD, 2026-11-16 (33 checks and the PC).
!
! INJECTION, DATA SIDE, OBSERVED 2026-11-16:
!   image bit 1 set, a zero word marked non-zero ("(16)9871498200400001"
!   -> "(16)D871498200400001", by --sub on the copy that runs; the
!   expected words are worked from the deck as it stands).  PREDICTED: the
!   zero SVEC[1] is kept second and the rest shift one place: SVEC+1..10.
!   OBSERVED: FAIL SVEC01-SVEC10 and nothing else ($3 ends at 24.0 either
!   way, and the last word loaded is the same).
!
! --- printed pp.37-38, Method 1 ---------------------------------------
START   LX, $1, XW1; LX, $3, XW3
        LVNI, $2, 1.0
        B, CONN
LOWF    LWF(U), SVEC ($2)
        ST(U), SVEC($3)
        V+IC, $3, 1.0
CONN    C0011(BU, 1) (V+IC), 0.01($1)
        BXCZ, END
        V+I, $2, 1.0
        BZRZ, LOWF
        B, CONN
END     Z, SVEC($3)                     !'zero unused region
        CB+, $3, END
        BEW, $
XW1     XW, BIMAGE, N+1, $
XW3     XW, 0.0, N, $
! --- the deck's: N, which the page does not define, and the data ------
N       SYN,24
        SLC, 199.0
GUARD1  DD(BU,64),(16)A5A5A5A5A5A5A5A5
BIMAGE  DD(BU,64),(16)9871498200400001
SVEC    DD(BU,64),(16)002C000000000000  !0
        DD(BU,64),(16)0000000000000000  !1
        DD(BU,64),(16)0000000000000000  !2
        DD(BU,64),(16)006A00000000000C  !3
        DD(BU,64),(16)0008000000000001  !4
        DD(BU,64),(16)0120000000000008  !5
        DD(BU,64),(16)0000000000000000  !6
        DD(BU,64),(16)0000000000000000  !7
        DD(BU,64),(16)0000000000000000  !8
        DD(BU,64),(16)00A0000C00000000  !9
        DD(BU,64),(16)005900000000000A  !10
        DD(BU,64),(16)014FFFFFFFFFFFF7  !11
        DD(BU,64),(16)0000000000000000  !12
        DD(BU,64),(16)0070000000000005  !13
        DD(BU,64),(16)0000000000000000  !14
        DD(BU,64),(16)004B000000000000  !15
        DD(BU,64),(16)0000000000000000  !16
        DD(BU,64),(16)00F0D55555555558  !17
        DD(BU,64),(16)0000000000000000  !18
        DD(BU,64),(16)0000000000000000  !19
        DD(BU,64),(16)002800000000000B  !20
        DD(BU,64),(16)0000000000000000  !21
        DD(BU,64),(16)0000000000000000  !22
        DD(BU,64),(16)00C9C0000000000E  !23
GUARD2  DD(BU,64),(16)5A5A5A5A5A5A5A5A
        END, START
